1. e4 { https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LO1Tkje_qvk&list=PLQKBpQZcRycrdFv_RC9rovYN1Wh6F_JPR&index=4 } 1... d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. f3 (4. Be3 { They want to attack with the Queen and Bishop. Also possible after f3. Don't castle to remove the purpose of their attack. You are playing c6 to counter their attack (NM Robert Ramirez uses other variation for explanation). } { [%csl Ge3,Gd2,Gf4][%cal Gd1d2,Gf2f3] }) 4... c6 { [%cal Gg2g4,Gb7b5] } 5. Be3 b5 6. Qd2 Qa5 { Castling is prevented by the thread of pawn to b4. You also make room for castling to the queen's side. } { [%csl Ga5,Gb5][%cal Ga5a2,Gb5b4,Ge8c8] } 7. g4 { [%cal Gh2h4,Gh4h5,Gg4g5] } (7. Bd3 { [%cal Ge1g1] } 7... Nbd7 8. Nge2 { You can now take it easy and castle or go for the knight attack again. } { [%cal Gd7b6,Gf8g7,Ge8g8] } 8... Nb6 9. O-O-O Nc4 10. Bxc4 bxc4) 7... Nbd7 { If they move the bishop to h6, just take it! } 8. h4 { Move is possible with knight or bishop. Both prepare a potential castling move. } { [%cal Gc8b7,Gd7b6,Ge8c8,Gb6c4,Ge3h6,Gf8h6] } 8... Nb6 9. h5 Nc4 { [%csl Ge3,Gd2][%cal Ge3h6,Gf1c4] } 10. Bxc4 bxc4 { [%cal Ga8b8,Gb8b2] } 11. O-O-O (11. hxg6 fxg6 12. Bh6 Bxh6 13. Qxh6 Rb8 { [%cal Ge1c1] } 14. Rb1 (14. O-O-O Qb4 { You have a strong attack after they castle. }) 14... Rxb2 15. Rxb2 Qxc3+ { [%cal Gc3b2] }) 11... Rb8 { [%cal Gb8b2] } *


