1. d4 g6 { Notice how he starts with g6 after d4. It's more of a modern defense style due to the start of the game. } 2. e4 d6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. Be2 { Relatively strange move. } { [%csl Ge2] } 4... Nf6 5. g4 { This is the start of the Spike Variation. } 5... c6 { Do you see something aggressive on the king's side? Just use c6. } 6. g5 Nfd7 { [%cal Gd7c5,Gb7b4,Ga6a3] } 7. h4 { They continue the attack but the king is not there. } { [%csl Ge8][%cal Gh4h6,Gg5g7] } 7... b5 8. h5 Rg8 { This provides space to hide the bishop on h8 after another pawn push. } 9. hxg6 hxg6 { [%cal Gh1h8] } 10. Nf3 b4 11. Nb1 a5 { Another way to create an attack instead of Qa5. } 12. a4 c5 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gg7a1] } 13. d5 { [%cal Gd1d3,Gd3h3] } 13... Nb6 { [%csl Ga4][%cal Gc5c4] } 14. c4 Kd7!! { Moving the King out of the center. Also connects the rook and the queen. } { [%cal Gd7c7,Gc7b7] } 15. Nbd2 Rh8 16. Rg1 Kc7 17. Rb1 { [%cal Gb2b3] } 17... Rh3 18. b3 Qh8 19. Nf1 N8d7 20. Bf4 { [%cal Gf4e5,Gf3e5] } 20... Ne5 21. Nxe5 Bxe5 22. Bxe5 Qxe5 { [%csl Ge5] } 23. f3 Bd7 { There is no counterplay for the opponent so why not finish your development. } { [%cal Ga8h8] } 24. Qc2 Qd4 25. Rg2 Rh1 26. Rf2 Qh8 { [%cal Gh8h4,Gd4h8,Gh4e1] } 27. f4 Qh4 { We were attacking from the beginning so they haven't had time to castle. } 28. Rd1 f6 29. gxf6 exf6 30. e5 fxe5 31. fxe5 Rf8 { [%csl Gf2][%cal Gf8f2,Gh4e1] } 32. exd6+ { Important, don't give your opponent chances by opening yourself to more checks by taking. } 32... Kb7 33. Bd3 Re8+ { 0-1 Black wins. } { [%cal Gd3e2] } 34. Be2 Rxf1+ 35. Kxf1 Qh1# 0-1


