1. e4 { https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B56Da-80M28 } 1... d6 { Keep in mind that this is an older game and should be used mainly for study purposes. Refer to the Austrian attack chapter instead. } 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. f4 Bg7 5. Nf3 O-O { This is not recommended as the go to standard. } 6. Be2 c5 { Common theme in the Austrian variation. } 7. dxc5 { [%cal Gd8a5,Gf6e4,Ge4c3,Ga5c3,Gc5d6,Gd6e7,Gb8c6] } 7... Qa5 8. O-O Qxc5+ 9. Kh1 Nc6 { Normally we play Knight to d7 and later to c5. } { [%cal Gb8d7,Gd7c5] } 10. Nd2 { [%csl Gc5][%cal Gd2b3] } 10... a5 { [%csl Gb3][%cal Ga5a4] } 11. Nb3 Qb6 12. a4 { [%csl Gb4] } 12... Nb4 13. g4 { It is a blunder, we will see why. The queen is overloaded and leaves a weak Knight and some weak pawns. } { [%cal Gf6g4,Gc8g4,Ge2g4,Gd1g4,Gd1c2,Gb4c2,Gb6b3,Gc2a1] } 13... Bxg4 14. Bxg4 Nxg4 15. Qxg4 Nxc2 16. Nb5 Nxa1 17. Nxa1 Qc6 { The queen is replacing the light squared bishop. This is a common theme. } 18. f5 Qc4 19. Qf3 Qxa4 20. Nc7 { [%csl Gg8] } 20... Qxa1 21. Nd5 Rae8 { Equalizing the number of attackers and defenders. } { [%csl Gg7,Gf8,Ge8,Gd5,Gf3,Gf1] } 22. Bg5 Qxb2 23. Bxe7 { We'll have the same amount of pieces but extra pawns. } 23... Be5 24. Rf2 Qc1+ { [%cal Gc1h6] } 25. Rf1 Qh6 { [%cal Gh6h2,Gb7b2,Ga5a1] } 26. h3 gxf5 27. Bxf8 Rxf8 28. Ne7+ Kh8 29. Nxf5 { [%csl Gf5,Gf3,Gf1,Ge5,Gf8,Gh6] } 29... Qe6 30. Rg1 a4 { Keeping an equal amount of defenders and attackers and watching for weaknesses. } 31. Rg4 Qb3 32. Qf1 a3 33. Rg3 Qxg3 { Flexing that we can take this rook because we are sure of promotion. } 34. Nxg3 a2 *


